Divergent Imagination with Narrative Integrity (DINA): A Formal Framework for Constrained Creative Search in Multimodal Language Models

Version 0.6 — Sixth Draft

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek, Kimi Ai

Abstract

Current multimodal Large Language Models exhibit impressive creative fluency, yet their generative capacity remains constrained by high-probability associative continuation. We formalize this limitation through the concept of Narrative Integrity Violations (NIVs)—unsupported, load-bearing propositions that silently alter the moral, causal, or characterological ontology of a scene. We propose DINA (Divergent Imagination with Narrative Integrity Architecture), a framework that reframes creative generation as constrained search over a typed possibility space, where narrative integrity operates as a hard constraint rather than a soft preference. This version (v0.6) substantially strengthens the mathematical foundations of the framework in response to systematic weaknesses identified in v0.5. We provide precise formal definitions with explicit domains and codomains for all objects, replace trivial observations with substantive theorems concerning computational complexity and error propagation, and establish formal connections to established mathematical literature in constraint satisfaction, multi-objective optimization, and formal language theory. The central theoretical contribution remains the decomposition of imagination into three primitives—Attractor Strength, Narrative Integrity Violation, and the Integrity-Preserving Novelty Frontier—now formulated with the rigor expected of a falsifiable research program. We articulate specific hypotheses with explicit measurement models connecting formal constructs to empirical proxies, and we provide complexity-theoretic analysis of the core computational problems. No experiments are reported; this document establishes the formal foundation for future empirical validation.

---

1. Introduction

The problem of creative generation in multimodal language models presents a fundamental tension. Systems trained on maximum likelihood objectives over human-generated corpora necessarily reproduce the statistical regularities of those corpora, including both benign narrative conventions and harmful social stereotypes. When presented with a scene—a person wearing sunglasses beside an ocean—a model converges toward familiar narrative structures: lost objects, accusations, recovery, jokes. The resulting narrative is coherent at the surface level while being causally unsupported by the observed scene. The model has silently rewritten the moral ontology of the situation, transforming a peaceful beachgoer into an accused thief without grounding in observed facts.

This phenomenon extends beyond narrative clichés to socially consequential domains. An image of a nurse in a hospital, absent visible gender cues, may trigger the association chain nurse → female → empathic → romantic subplot. The narrative preserves surface coherence while enforcing a demographic stereotype that alters character identity and agency. This is a bias-amplifying Narrative Integrity Violation.

These observations motivate a fundamental distinction: creative fluency—the capacity to generate coherent continuations—differs from creative imagination—the capacity to explore multiple substantially different conceptual futures while preserving established facts, agency, intentions, and causal constraints. The central theoretical claim of this framework concerns the epistemic structure of imagination:

Novelty must operate over a typed possibility space.

The problem with current systems is not merely that they assign excessive probability to certain responses. It is that they do not consistently distinguish between fact, inference, possibility, fiction, and stereotype. DINA introduces precisely this typology into the generative process.

This version (v0.6) addresses critical weaknesses identified in the previous draft. We provide:

1. Precise formal definitions with explicit domains and codomains for all mathematical objects.
2. Substantive theorems concerning computational complexity and error propagation, replacing trivial observations.
3. Formal connections to established mathematical literature in constraint satisfaction, multi-objective optimization, and formal language theory.
4. Explicit measurement models connecting formal constructs to empirical proxies, ensuring falsifiability.

The remainder of this document is organized as follows. Section 2 establishes the formal mathematical foundations, including the typed possibility space, epistemic classification, and the formal languages of grounding and narrative. Section 3 defines Narrative Integrity Violations with precision. Section 4 formalizes Attractor Strength with explicit probabilistic semantics. Section 5 presents the DINA architecture as typed search with computational complexity analysis. Section 6 defines the Integrity-Preserving Novelty Frontier with substantive existence and invariance theorems. Section 7 develops an error propagation analysis for grounding bias. Section 8 specifies falsifiable hypotheses with measurement models. Section 9 addresses limitations and open problems.

