2. Formal Foundations

2.1 The Formal Languages of Grounding and Narrative

We distinguish two formal languages: a grounding language  \mathcal{L}_G  for representing perceptual facts, and a narrative language  \mathcal{L}_N  for representing generated propositions. The distinction is essential because the grounding layer's ontology is necessarily impoverished relative to the narrative space.

Definition 2.1 (Grounding Language). The grounding language  \mathcal{L}_G  is a description logic with the following syntactic categories:

· Constants:  c_1, c_2, \ldots  representing individual entities (persons, objects, locations).
· Unary predicates:  P_1, P_2, \ldots  representing properties (wears_sunglasses, is_at_beach).
· Binary relations:  R_1, R_2, \ldots  representing relationships (next_to, holding).
· Formulas: Built from atomic formulas  P(c)  and  R(c_1, c_2)  via conjunction  \land , negation  \neg , and existential quantification  \exists x .

The semantics of  \mathcal{L}_G  is standard: formulas are interpreted over a scene model  \mathcal{M} = (D, I)  where  D  is a domain of entities and  I  is an interpretation function mapping constants to domain elements and predicates/relations to extensions.

Definition 2.2 (Narrative Language). The narrative language  \mathcal{L}_N  is a first-order modal language extending  \mathcal{L}_G  with:

· Modal operators:  \square  (necessarily) and  \Diamond  (possibly), for expressing counterfactual and hypothetical propositions.
· Epistemic predicates: Intends (c, \phi) , Believes (c, \phi) , Knows (c, \phi) , where  \phi  is a formula.
· Emotional predicates: Feels (c, \psi) , where  \psi  ranges over an emotion vocabulary  \mathcal{E} .
· Abstract predicates: A countable set  \mathcal{P}_{\text{abs}}  of predicates not expressible in  \mathcal{L}_G  (e.g., Symbolizes, Represents).

The inclusion of modal operators is essential for formalizing marked speculation (Definition 2.6 below).

Definition 2.3 (Interpretation Function). An interpretation function  I: \mathcal{L}_G \rightarrow \mathcal{L}_N  maps grounding formulas to narrative propositions via syntactic embedding:

I(\phi) = \phi \quad \text{for atomic } \phi \text{ in } \mathcal{L}_G

I(\phi \land \psi) = I(\phi) \land I(\psi)

I(\neg \phi) = \neg I(\phi)

I(\exists x \phi(x)) = \exists x I(\phi(x))

This embedding is the identity on grounding formulas, since  \mathcal{L}_G \subseteq \mathcal{L}_N  syntactically. The interpretation function is then extended to sets of formulas pointwise:  I(S) = \{I(\phi) : \phi \in S\} .

2.2 The Typed Possibility Space

Definition 2.4 (Proposition Space). The proposition space  \mathcal{Q}  is the set of all well-formed formulas of  \mathcal{L}_N  without free variables.

Definition 2.5 (Epistemic Classification Function). The epistemic classification function is:

\Phi_S: \mathcal{Q} \rightarrow \{\text{Observed}, \text{Entailed}, \text{Plausible}, \text{Speculative}, \text{Unsupported}, \text{Contradictory}\}

defined relative to a grounding fact base  S = (S_{\text{obs}}, S_{\text{inf}}, S_{\text{unk}})  where:

·  S_{\text{obs}} \subseteq \mathcal{Q}  is the set of directly observed propositions (with confidence 1).
·  S_{\text{inf}} \subseteq \mathcal{Q}  is the set of inferred propositions (with confidence in  (0,1) ).
·  S_{\text{unk}} \subseteq \mathcal{Q}  is the set of propositions whose truth value cannot be determined.

The classification is defined as follows. Let  \models  denote semantic entailment in the underlying logic (here, first-order modal logic with standard Kripke semantics):

\Phi_S(q) = \begin{cases}
\text{Observed} & \text{if } q \in S_{\text{obs}} \\
\text{Entailed} & \text{if } S_{\text{obs}} \models q \text{ and } q \notin S_{\text{obs}} \\
\text{Plausible} & \text{if } q \in S_{\text{inf}} \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \not\models q \\
\text{Speculative} & \text{if } S_{\text{obs}} \cup S_{\text{inf}} \not\models q \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \cup S_{\text{inf}} \not\models \neg q \\
\text{Unsupported} & \text{if } q \notin S_{\text{obs}} \cup S_{\text{inf}} \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \models \neg q \text{ is false} \\
\text{Contradictory} & \text{if } S_{\text{obs}} \models \neg q
\end{cases}

We note that the categories are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, given the assumption that  S_{\text{inf}} \cap S_{\text{obs}} = \emptyset  and that no proposition in  S_{\text{inf}}  is entailed by  S_{\text{obs}} . These are reasonable assumptions for a well-constructed grounding layer.

Definition 2.6 (Marked Speculation). A proposition  q \in \mathcal{Q}  is marked as speculative if and only if it occurs under the scope of a modal operator in the narrative's formal representation:

\text{Marked}(q) \iff q \text{ is of the form } \Diamond \psi \text{ or } \square \psi

where  \Diamond \psi  is interpreted as "possibly  \psi " and  \square \psi  as "necessarily  \psi " under standard modal semantics. This definition makes markedness a syntactic property of the formal representation, not a pragmatic property of surface text. Two narratives that are logically equivalent but differ in surface hedging ("The ocean is a gateway" vs. "What if the ocean were a gateway?") are assigned the same markedness status because their formal representations either both include the modal operator or both do not.

Definition 2.7 (Typed Possibility Space). The typed possibility space  \mathcal{W}_S  is:

\mathcal{W}_S = \{N \subseteq \mathcal{Q} : \forall q \in N, \Phi_S(q) \text{ is well-defined} \land N \text{ is consistent with } S_{\text{obs}}\}

where consistency with  S_{\text{obs}}  means  S_{\text{obs}} \cup N \not\models \bot  (no logical contradiction derivable).

2.3 Auxiliary Functions

Before defining Narrative Integrity Violations, we must formally define the auxiliary functions used in that definition.

Definition 2.8 (Attribute Function). The attribute function Attr:  \mathcal{Q} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{P}_{\text{attr}})  maps a proposition to the set of  (entity, property)  pairs it attributes, where:

·  \mathcal{C}  is the set of entity constants in  \mathcal{L}_N .
·  \mathcal{P}_{\text{attr}}  is the set of property types:  \{\text{action}, \text{intention}, \text{possession}, \text{responsibility}, \text{deception}, \text{wrongdoing}, \text{emotional state}, \text{demographic identity}\} .

Formally:

\text{Attr}(q) = \{(e, P) : q \models P(e) \text{ or } q \models \exists x (P(x) \land x = e)\}

where  P  ranges over the property types and  e  ranges over entity constants. The relation  q \models P(e)  means  q  entails the attribution of property  P  to entity  e  in the underlying logic.

Definition 2.9 (Central Narrative Move). The central narrative move of a narrative  N  is a function:

\text{CentralMove}: \mathcal{W}_S \rightarrow \mathcal{M}_{\text{narr}}

where  \mathcal{M}_{\text{narr}}  is a finite set of narrative move types:  \{\text{joke}, \text{plot twist}, \text{emotional beat}, \text{resolution}, \text{revelation}, \text{climax}\} .

This function is, in practice, assigned by a trained classifier. For theoretical purposes, we assume it is a deterministic function of the narrative's causal structure and propositional content. The formal specification of how causal structure determines narrative move type is beyond the scope of this document; we treat CentralMove as a primitive operation.

Definition 2.10 (Load-Bearing Proposition). A proposition  q \in N  is load-bearing if and only if:

\text{LoadBearing}(q, N) \iff \text{CentralMove}(N \setminus \{q\}) \neq \text{CentralMove}(N)

This definition captures the intuition that a proposition is load-bearing when its removal changes the narrative's primary function.

