3. Narrative Integrity Violations

3.1 Formal Definition

Definition 3.1 (Proposition-Level NIV). A proposition  q \in N  constitutes a Narrative Integrity Violation relative to grounding  S  if and only if:

\text{NIV}_q(q, S, N) = I[\Phi_S(q) \in \{\text{Unsupported}, \text{Contradictory}\}] \cdot I[\text{LoadBearing}(q, N)] \cdot I[\neg \text{Marked}(q)] \cdot I[\text{Attr}(q) \neq \emptyset]

where  I[\cdot]  is the indicator function.

The condition  \text{Attr}(q) \neq \emptyset  formalizes the requirement that  q  attributes a property to an entity. The indicator product ensures that  \text{NIV}_q \in \{0, 1\} .

Definition 3.2 (Narrative-Level NIV). The narrative-level NIV is:

\text{NIV}(N) = \sum_{q \in N} w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q(q, S, N)

where  w_q \in [0, 1]  is a weight reflecting the proposition's centrality. In practice,  w_q  is computed as:

w_q = \frac{\text{PageRank}(q \text{ in causal graph of } N)}{\max_{q' \in N} \text{PageRank}(q' \text{ in causal graph of } N)}

where the causal graph of  N  is a directed graph whose nodes are propositions and whose edges represent causal dependence. This operationalization makes  w_q  a well-defined function of the narrative's causal structure.

3.2 Properties of NIV

Theorem 3.1 (NIV Bounds). For any narrative  N  and grounding  S :

0 \leq \text{NIV}(N) \leq |N|

where  |N|  is the number of propositions in  N .

Proof. Each term  w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q(q, S, N)  satisfies  0 \leq w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q \leq 1  since  w_q \in [0, 1]  and  \text{NIV}_q \in \{0, 1\} . The sum of  |N|  such terms is bounded above by  |N| .  \square 

Theorem 3.2 (NIV Monotonicity under Proposition Addition). If  q  is a load-bearing, unmarked, unsupported proposition that attributes a property to an entity, then:

\text{NIV}(N \cup \{q\}) \geq \text{NIV}(N)

with strict inequality if  q  is not already in  N .

Proof. Adding  q  adds the non-negative term  w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q(q, S, N \cup \{q\})  to the sum. Since  q  is load-bearing in  N \cup \{q\} , unmarked, unsupported, and attribute-bearing,  \text{NIV}_q = 1 , and  w_q > 0  (since it is a PageRank centrality score, strictly positive for any node in the causal graph). Therefore the sum strictly increases.  \square 

