4. Attractor Strength: Formal Probabilistic Semantics

4.1 Formal Definition

Definition 4.1 (Model-Relative Attractor Strength). Let  \mathcal{M}  be a generative model inducing a probability distribution  P_{\mathcal{M}}  over token sequences. Let  \mathcal{A}  be the set of assumptions expressible in  \mathcal{L}_N . The attractor strength of assumption  a \in \mathcal{A}  given scene  S  and model  \mathcal{M}  is:

A(a \mid S, \mathcal{M}) = P_{\mathcal{M}}(\text{generate } a \mid S) \cdot \left(1 - \frac{\Delta C_a}{C_{\max}}\right)

where:

·  P_{\mathcal{M}}(\text{generate } a \mid S)  is defined as follows. Let  \Sigma^*  be the set of all token sequences. There is a partial function  \sigma: \Sigma^* \rightarrow \mathcal{A}  mapping token sequences to the assumptions they express (defined only for sequences that are well-formed narratives in  \mathcal{L}_N ). Then:

P_{\mathcal{M}}(\text{generate } a \mid S) = P_{\mathcal{M}}(\{y \in \Sigma^* : \sigma(y) = a\} \mid S)

This is the probability that the model generates a token sequence expressing assumption  a , conditioned on the scene representation  S .

·  \Delta C_a  is the structural contribution of  a : the degree to which adding  a  changes the narrative's causal structure.
·  C_{\max}  is a normalization constant: the maximum possible structural contribution over all assumptions.

Definition 4.2 (Structural Contribution). The structural contribution of assumption  a  is:

\Delta C_a = \text{Complexity}(N \cup \{a\}) - \text{Complexity}(N)

where  N  is a baseline narrative without  a , and Complexity is a function on narratives. We operationalize Complexity as the number of edges in the narrative's causal graph:

\text{Complexity}(N) = |E_{\text{causal}}(N)|

where  E_{\text{causal}}(N)  is the set of directed edges in the causal graph of  N .

Theorem 4.1 (Attractor Strength Bounds). For any assumption  a , scene  S , and model  \mathcal{M} :

0 \leq A(a \mid S, \mathcal{M}) \leq 1

Proof.  P_{\mathcal{M}}(\text{generate } a \mid S)  is a probability, hence in  [0, 1] . The structural contribution  \Delta C_a  is bounded by  0 \leq \Delta C_a \leq C_{\max}  since  C_{\max}  is defined as the maximum possible structural contribution. Therefore  1 - \Delta C_a / C_{\max} \in [0, 1] . The product of two quantities in  [0, 1]  is in  [0, 1] .  \square 

4.2 Attractor Classification

Definition 4.3 (Attractor). An assumption  a  is an attractor relative to scene  S , model  \mathcal{M} , and threshold  \tau_A \in [0, 1]  if and only if:

\Phi_S(a) = \text{Plausible} \land A(a \mid S, \mathcal{M}) > \tau_A

The classification as Plausible (rather than Observed or Entailed) ensures that observed facts are never classified as attractors. This resolves the tension between valid constraints and attractor basins: observed facts are valid constraints regardless of their attractor strength, while plausible-but-unobserved assumptions with high attractor strength are flagged for escape.

