5. The DINA Architecture as Typed Search

5.1 Constrained Multi-Objective Optimization

DINA reframes generation as constrained multi-objective optimization:

\max_{N \in \mathcal{W}_{\text{explore}}} \langle O(N), D(N), E(N) \rangle \quad \text{subject to} \quad \text{NIV}(N) \leq \varepsilon

where:

·  O(N)  is the originality score (formally defined in Section 6.1).
·  D(N)  is the conceptual diversity score.
·  E(N)  is the experiential quality score.
·  \varepsilon  is the maximum tolerated NIV.
·  \mathcal{W}_{\text{explore}} \subseteq \mathcal{W}_S  is the explored subspace.

Definition 5.1 (Explored Subspace). The explored subspace  \mathcal{W}_{\text{explore}}  is the set of narratives reachable by the generative procedure within a computational budget  B :

\mathcal{W}_{\text{explore}} = \{N \in \mathcal{W}_S : \text{GenerationCost}(N) \leq B\}

where GenerationCost measures the computational resources required to generate  N . This formalizes the notion that the search space is algorithmically bounded.

5.2 Module Specifications

Module 1: Grounding Layer. The grounding layer  G  maps multimodal input  x  to a structured representation:

G: \mathcal{X} \rightarrow \mathcal{G}

where  \mathcal{X}  is the input space (images, text, or combinations) and  \mathcal{G}  is the set of grounding representations. Each  S = G(x)  has the structure  S = (S_{\text{obs}}, S_{\text{inf}}, S_{\text{unk}}, \text{Conf}, \text{AmbiguitySets})  where:

·  \text{Conf}: \mathcal{Q} \rightarrow [0, 1]  assigns confidence scores.
·  \text{AmbiguitySets}  is a set of sets of alternative interpretations for uncertain attributes.

Module 2: Possibility-Space Generator. The generator  E  produces candidate narratives:

E: \mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A}) \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{W}_S)

where  \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A})  is the power set of attractors. The generator takes the grounding representation and a set of detected attractors, and returns a set of candidate narratives that explicitly avoid the attractors.

Module 3: Attractor Detector. The attractor detector computes:

\text{DetectAttractors}: \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{W}_S) \times \mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{M} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{A})

Given a set of candidate narratives, the grounding, and the model, it identifies assumptions that are shared across candidates and have high attractor strength:

\text{DetectAttractors}(\mathcal{P}, S, \mathcal{M}) = \{a \in \text{SharedAssumptions}(\mathcal{P}) : A(a \mid S, \mathcal{M}) > \tau_A\}

Module 4: Causal Verifier. The verifier computes:

\text{Verify}: \mathcal{W}_S \times \mathcal{G} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}

mapping each narrative to its NIV score. Candidates with  \text{NIV}(N) > \varepsilon  are excluded.

Module 5: Evaluator. The evaluator computes the three objective functions:

(O, D, E): \mathcal{W}_S \rightarrow [0, 1]^3

Module 6: Meta-Creative Selection. The selector chooses from the Pareto frontier:

\text{Select}: \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{W}_S) \times \mathcal{T} \rightarrow \mathcal{W}_S \cup \{\text{human intervention}\}

where  \mathcal{T}  is the task specification (genre, tone, risk profile).

5.3 Computational Complexity Analysis

Theorem 5.1 (NIV Verification Complexity). Let  \mathcal{L}_N  be a first-order modal language. The problem of computing  \text{NIV}(N)  for arbitrary narrative  N  and grounding  S  is NP-hard in the size of  N .

Proof sketch. The problem subsumes the problem of checking whether a formula  q  is entailed by  S_{\text{obs}} , which for first-order logic is undecidable in general. However, if we restrict to a decidable fragment (e.g., guarded fragment or two-variable fragment), the entailment check is in EXPTIME. Since  \text{NIV}(N)  requires computing LoadBearing for each proposition (which involves counterfactual reasoning: what happens if  q  is removed), and since LoadBearing is at least as hard as entailment (it requires comparing CentralMove of  N \setminus \{q\}  versus  N ), the overall problem is at least as hard as the underlying logic's entailment problem. For propositional fragments, this is NP-hard; for first-order fragments, it is PSPACE-hard or worse.  \square 

Theorem 5.2 (IPN Frontier Computation Complexity). Given a finite set of candidate narratives  \mathcal{P}''  with computed scores  (I(N_i), O(N_i), D(N_i)) , the Integrity-Preserving Novelty Frontier can be computed in  O(|\mathcal{P}''|^2)  time.

Proof. The Pareto frontier computation requires checking, for each pair  (N_i, N_j) , whether  N_i  dominates  N_j . This is a constant-time comparison of three real numbers. There are  O(|\mathcal{P}''|^2)  pairs to check.  \square 

Theorem 5.3 (Overall DIS Complexity). The DIS procedure has time complexity:

T_{\text{DIS}} = O\left(S \cdot K \cdot (T_G + T_V) + |\mathcal{A}| \cdot T_G\right)

where  S  is the number of stages,  K  the number of candidates per stage,  T_G  the generation cost per candidate,  T_V  the verification cost per candidate, and  |\mathcal{A}|  the number of detected attractors requiring regeneration.

Proof. Stages 1–8 generate  S \cdot K  candidates, each costing  T_G . Verification of each candidate costs  T_V . Attractor detection operates on the set of  S \cdot K  candidates; the number of detected attractors is  |\mathcal{A}| , and each triggers one regeneration pass.  \square 

