6. The Integrity-Preserving Novelty Frontier

6.1 Score Definitions

Definition 6.1 (Originality Score). The originality score is:

O(N) = 1 - \max_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{known}}} \cos\_\text{sim}(\text{embed}(N), \text{embed}(N_a))

where  \mathcal{A}_{\text{known}}  is the set of known attractor narratives,  N_a  is the canonical narrative associated with attractor  a , embed is a fixed sentence encoder mapping narratives to a vector space  \mathbb{R}^d , and  \cos\_\text{sim}  is cosine similarity. This definition makes originality a well-defined function  O: \mathcal{W}_S \rightarrow [0, 1] .

Definition 6.2 (Conceptual Diversity Score). The conceptual diversity of a set of branches  \mathcal{P}  is:

D(\mathcal{P}) = \frac{2}{|\mathcal{P}|(|\mathcal{P}|-1)} \sum_{i < j} d_c(p_i, p_j)

where  d_c  is the conceptual distance metric. We operationalize  d_c  as:

d_c(p_i, p_j) = 1 - \cos\_\text{sim}(\text{embed}(p_i), \text{embed}(p_j)) + \lambda \cdot \text{GraphEditDistance}(G_{\text{causal}}(p_i), G_{\text{causal}}(p_j))

where  \lambda > 0  is a weighting parameter and GraphEditDistance measures the structural difference between causal graphs.

Definition 6.3 (Integrity Score). The integrity score is:

I(N) = \frac{1}{1 + \text{NIV}(N)}

mapping  \text{NIV} \in [0, |N|]  to  I \in [\frac{1}{1+|N|}, 1] .

6.2 Pareto Dominance and Frontier

Definition 6.4 (Pareto Dominance). Narrative  N_i  dominates  N_j  on dimensions  (I, O, D)  if and only if:

I(N_i) \geq I(N_j) \land O(N_i) \geq O(N_j) \land D(N_i) \geq D(N_j)

with strict inequality in at least one dimension.

Definition 6.5 (Integrity-Preserving Novelty Frontier). The IPN frontier is:

\mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}} = \{N_i \in \mathcal{P}'' : \nexists N_j \in \mathcal{P}'' \text{ such that } N_j \text{ dominates } N_i\}

Theorem 6.1 (Frontier Non-Emptiness). If  \mathcal{P}''  is non-empty and finite, then  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}  is non-empty.

Proof. We prove by induction on  |\mathcal{P}''| . Base case  |\mathcal{P}''| = 1 : the single element is trivially non-dominated. Inductive step: assume the theorem holds for  |\mathcal{P}''| = k , and consider adding  N_{k+1} . Either  N_{k+1}  is dominated by some element of  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^k  (the frontier of the first  k  elements), in which case  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^{k+1} = \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^k  (non-empty by induction hypothesis); or  N_{k+1}  is not dominated, in which case it is added to the frontier; or  N_{k+1}  dominates some existing frontier elements, which are removed but  N_{k+1}  is added. In all cases the frontier remains non-empty.  \square 

Theorem 6.2 (Frontier Invariance under Monotone Transformations). If  \phi: \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}  is strictly increasing, then the Pareto dominance relation is invariant under applying  \phi  to any single dimension.

Proof. Suppose  N_i  dominates  N_j  on  (I, O, D) . Then  I(N_i) \geq I(N_j) ,  O(N_i) \geq O(N_j) ,  D(N_i) \geq D(N_j)  with strict inequality in at least one dimension. If  \phi  is strictly increasing, then  \phi(I(N_i)) \geq \phi(I(N_j))  with equality if and only if  I(N_i) = I(N_j) , and similarly for the other dimensions. Therefore the dominance relation is preserved.  \square 

Theorem 6.3 (NIV Stability under Grounding Perturbation). Let  S  and  S'  be two grounding representations with Hamming distance  d(S, S') = k  (the number of propositions classified differently). Then for any narrative  N :

|\text{NIV}_S(N) - \text{NIV}_{S'}(N)| \leq k

Proof. Each proposition  q \in N  contributes  w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q(q, S, N)  to the NIV sum. A change in grounding  S  can affect  \text{NIV}_q  only if the epistemic classification  \Phi_S(q)  changes. Since  d(S, S') = k , at most  k  propositions change classification. For each such proposition, the NIV contribution changes by at most  w_q \leq 1 . Therefore the total change is bounded by  k .  \square 

This theorem provides a formal stability guarantee: small grounding errors produce bounded NIV errors.

