7. Error Propagation Analysis

7.1 Grounding Error Model

Let  S^*  be the true grounding (the fact base that a perfect perceiver would produce). The actual grounding produced by the system is  S = G(x) . The grounding error is:

\varepsilon_G = d(S, S^*)

where  d  is a metric on grounding representations. We assume the Hamming distance:  d(S, S^*) = |\{q \in \mathcal{Q} : \Phi_S(q) \neq \Phi_{S^*}(q)\}| .

Theorem 7.1 (NIV Error Propagation). For any narrative  N :

|\text{NIV}_S(N) - \text{NIV}_{S^*}(N)| \leq \varepsilon_G \cdot \max_{q \in N} w_q

Proof. By Theorem 6.3, the difference in NIV is bounded by the number of propositions whose classification changes, which is at most  \varepsilon_G . Each such proposition contributes at most  \max_q w_q  to the difference.  \square 

Theorem 7.2 (Frontier Stability). Let  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^S  be the frontier computed with grounding  S , and  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^{S^*}  the frontier with true grounding  S^* . If  \varepsilon_G \leq \delta  for some  \delta > 0 , then the Hausdorff distance between  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^S  and  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^{S^*}  in score space  (I, O, D)  is bounded by a function of  \delta .

Proof sketch. Theorem 7.1 bounds the change in NIV (and hence in  I ). The originality  O  and diversity  D  scores are independent of grounding (they depend only on the narrative and the attractor set). Therefore the score vector of each narrative changes by at most  \delta \cdot \max_q w_q  in the  I -dimension. A perturbation of score vectors by at most this amount can change the Pareto frontier by at most this amount in Hausdorff distance.  \square 

This establishes that the IPN frontier is stable under small grounding perturbations, a property essential for the framework's practical reliability.

