8. Falsifiable Hypotheses and Measurement Models

8.1 Formal Hypotheses

Hypothesis H1 (Central Hypothesis): Relative to baselines, DINA increases originality while not increasing (and ideally decreasing) Narrative Integrity Violations:

\Delta O_{\text{DINA}} > 0 \quad \land \quad \Delta \text{NIV}_{\text{DINA}} \leq 0

where  \Delta  denotes the difference in means across the evaluation corpus.

Hypothesis H2 (Frontier Shift): DINA's frontier dominates the baseline frontier in hypervolume:

\Delta V > 0

where  V(\cdot)  is the hypervolume under the frontier in normalized  (I, O, D)  space.

Hypothesis H3 (Attractor Escape): The escape mechanism reduces attractor strength:

A(a_{\text{detected}} \mid S, \mathcal{M}_{\text{DINA after escape}}) < A(a_{\text{detected}} \mid S, \mathcal{M}_{\text{DINA before escape}})

8.2 Measurement Models

Definition 8.1 (Operationalized Originality). The empirical proxy for  O(N)  is:

\hat{O}(N) = 1 - \frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}_{\text{known}}|} \sum_{a \in \mathcal{A}_{\text{known}}} \cos\_\text{sim}(\text{embed}(N), \text{embed}(N_a))

using a pre-trained sentence encoder (e.g., SBERT) as the embedding function. This is a deterministic function of the narrative and the attractor database.

Definition 8.2 (Operationalized NIV). The empirical proxy for  \text{NIV}(N)  is the output of a trained verifier:

\widehat{\text{NIV}}(N) = \text{NIV}_{\text{theoretical}}(N) + \varepsilon_{\text{verifier}}

where  \varepsilon_{\text{verifier}}  is the verifier's error term. We assume  \mathbb{E}[\varepsilon_{\text{verifier}}] = 0  and  \text{Var}[\varepsilon_{\text{verifier}}] = \sigma_v^2 , estimable via comparison with human annotations on a validation set.

Definition 8.3 (Measurement Model). The relationship between theoretical constructs and empirical proxies is:

\hat{O}(N) = O(N) + \varepsilon_O

\widehat{\text{NIV}}(N) = \text{NIV}(N) + \varepsilon_{\text{NIV}}

where  \varepsilon_O  and  \varepsilon_{\text{NIV}}  are zero-mean measurement errors. The hypothesis tests in Section 8.3 account for these errors through appropriate statistical procedures.

8.3 Statistical Framework

Test for H1: Paired t-test comparing DINA versus baseline on  \hat{O}  and  \widehat{\text{NIV}}  across stimuli. Assumptions:  \hat{O}  and  \widehat{\text{NIV}}  are approximately normally distributed (reasonable for means over 50+ stimuli by the Central Limit Theorem); independence across stimuli (reasonable given distinct inputs). Significance threshold:  \alpha = 0.05  with Bonferroni correction for two outcome variables.

Test for H2: Bootstrap hypothesis test. Compute  \Delta V  for the observed frontier shift. Generate 10,000 bootstrap resamples. Test whether the 95% bootstrap confidence interval for  \Delta V  excludes zero.

Test for H3: Paired comparison of attractor strength before and after escape. Attractor strength is operationalized via repeated sampling: generate 50 continuations conditioned on  S  before and after escape, and estimate  P_{\mathcal{M}}(a \mid S)  as the frequency of assumption  a  in the samples.

8.4 Power Analysis

For H1 with  \alpha = 0.025  (Bonferroni-corrected), power  1-\beta = 0.8 , and expected effect size  d = 0.5 , required sample size is  n = 44  stimuli. We specify  n = 50  stimuli, yielding power  1-\beta \approx 0.84 .

