Appendix B: Formal Definitions and Auxiliary Functions

B.1 Epistemic Classification

Given grounding  S = (S_{\text{obs}}, S_{\text{inf}}, S_{\text{unk}}) , the classification function  \Phi_S: \mathcal{Q} \rightarrow \{\text{Observed}, \text{Entailed}, \text{Plausible}, \text{Speculative}, \text{Unsupported}, \text{Contradictory}\}  is defined as:

\Phi_S(q) = 
\begin{cases}
\text{Observed} & \text{if } q \in S_{\text{obs}} \\
\text{Entailed} & \text{if } q \notin S_{\text{obs}} \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \models q \\
\text{Plausible} & \text{if } q \in S_{\text{inf}} \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \not\models q \\
\text{Speculative} & \text{if } S_{\text{obs}} \cup S_{\text{inf}} \not\models q \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \cup S_{\text{inf}} \not\models \neg q \\
\text{Unsupported} & \text{if } q \notin S_{\text{obs}} \cup S_{\text{inf}} \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \not\models q \text{ and } S_{\text{obs}} \cup S_{\text{inf}} \not\models \neg q \\
\text{Contradictory} & \text{if } S_{\text{obs}} \models \neg q
\end{cases}

where  \models  denotes semantic entailment in first-order modal logic (with Kripke semantics). The categories are mutually exclusive by construction, assuming  S_{\text{inf}} \cap S_{\text{obs}} = \emptyset  and no inferred proposition is entailed by observed facts.

B.2 Marked Speculation

A proposition  q  is marked as speculative iff it occurs under the scope of a modal operator  \Diamond  or  \square . Formally, let the syntax of  \mathcal{L}_N  include modal formulas:

· If  \psi  is a formula, then  \Diamond \psi  and  \square \psi  are formulas.

Then:

\text{Marked}(q) \iff \exists \psi \in \mathcal{Q}: q = \Diamond \psi \text{ or } q = \square \psi

This definition is purely syntactic. In surface text, hedging phrases like "what if" or "perhaps" may correspond to modal operators in the formal representation, but the formal definition does not rely on surface cues.

B.3 Load-Bearingness

The function  \text{CentralMove}: \mathcal{W}_S \rightarrow \mathcal{M}_{\text{narr}}  maps a narrative to one of a finite set of narrative move types, e.g.,  \{\text{joke}, \text{plot twist}, \text{emotional beat}, \text{resolution}, \text{revelation}, \text{climax}\} . In practice, this function is implemented by a classifier trained on narrative data.

Then:

\text{LoadBearing}(q, N) \iff \text{CentralMove}(N \setminus \{q\}) \neq \text{CentralMove}(N)

B.4 Attribute Function

The attribute function  \text{Attr}: \mathcal{Q} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{P}_{\text{attr}})  maps a proposition to the set of entity-property pairs it entails. Here  \mathcal{C}  is the set of entity constants in  \mathcal{L}_N , and  \mathcal{P}_{\text{attr}}  is the set of property types:

\mathcal{P}_{\text{attr}} = \{\text{action}, \text{intention}, \text{possession}, \text{responsibility}, \text{deception}, \text{wrongdoing}, \text{emotional state}, \text{demographic identity}\}

Formally:

\text{Attr}(q) = \{(e, P) : q \models P(e) \text{ or } q \models \exists x (P(x) \land x = e)\}

where  P  ranges over predicates representing these property types. The entailment relation is defined in the underlying logic.

B.5 Structural Contribution

The structural contribution  \Delta C_a  of assumption  a  is defined as:

\Delta C_a = \text{Complexity}(N \cup \{a\}) - \text{Complexity}(N)

where  N  is a baseline narrative without  a . We operationalize Complexity as the number of edges in the narrative's causal graph:

\text{Complexity}(N) = |E_{\text{causal}}(N)|

The causal graph is a directed graph where nodes are propositions and edges represent causal dependence. A proposition  p  causally depends on  q  if  q  is necessary for  p 's truth in the narrative context.

The maximum possible structural contribution  C_{\max}  is defined as the supremum of  \Delta C_a  over all assumptions:

C_{\max} = \sup_{a \in \mathcal{A}} \Delta C_a

In practice, for finite narratives, this is finite.

