Appendix C: Proofs of Key Theorems

C.1 Theorem 3.1 (NIV Bounds)

Statement: For any narrative  N  and grounding  S ,  0 \leq \text{NIV}(N) \leq |N| .

Proof. By Definition 3.2,

\text{NIV}(N) = \sum_{q \in N} w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q(q, S, N)

Each  w_q \in [0,1]  and  \text{NIV}_q \in \{0,1\} , so each term  w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q \in [0,1] . Summing over  |N|  terms yields  0 \leq \text{NIV}(N) \leq |N| .  \square 

C.2 Theorem 3.2 (NIV Monotonicity under Proposition Addition)

Statement: If  q  is a load-bearing, unmarked, unsupported proposition that attributes a property to an entity, then  \text{NIV}(N \cup \{q\}) \geq \text{NIV}(N) , with strict inequality if  q \notin N .

Proof. Let  N' = N \cup \{q\} . By Definition 3.1,  \text{NIV}_q(q, S, N') = 1  because:

·  \Phi_S(q) \in \{\text{Unsupported}, \text{Contradictory}\}  (given unsupported).
·  \text{LoadBearing}(q, N')  holds (given).
·  \neg \text{Marked}(q)  holds (given unmarked).
·  \text{Attr}(q) \neq \emptyset  (given attributes property).

Thus the term corresponding to  q  in the sum is  w_q \cdot 1 = w_q . Since  w_q  is the PageRank centrality of  q  in the causal graph of  N' , and  q  is part of the graph,  w_q > 0 . All other terms remain unchanged (their  \text{NIV}_q  may change if the addition of  q  alters load-bearingness of other propositions, but each term is non-negative). Therefore,

\text{NIV}(N') = \text{NIV}(N) + w_q + \sum_{\substack{q' \in N \\ q' \neq q}} \Delta_{q'} \geq \text{NIV}(N) + w_q > \text{NIV}(N)

where  \Delta_{q'}  accounts for any changes in other terms (which could be positive or negative, but the sum of all other changes plus  w_q  is strictly positive since  w_q > 0 ).  \square 

C.3 Theorem 4.1 (Attractor Strength Bounds)

Statement: For any assumption  a , scene  S , and model  \mathcal{M} ,  0 \leq A(a \mid S, \mathcal{M}) \leq 1 .

Proof.  P_{\mathcal{M}}(\text{generate } a \mid S) \in [0,1]  because it is a probability. The structural contribution  \Delta C_a \geq 0  and by definition  \Delta C_a \leq C_{\max} , so  0 \leq 1 - \frac{\Delta C_a}{C_{\max}} \leq 1 . The product of two numbers in  [0,1]  is in  [0,1] .  \square 

C.4 Theorem 5.1 (NIV Verification Complexity)

Statement: The problem of computing  \text{NIV}(N)  for arbitrary narrative  N  and grounding  S  is NP-hard in the size of  N .

Proof sketch. We reduce from SAT. Let  \phi  be a propositional formula in CNF. We construct a narrative  N  consisting of a single load-bearing proposition  q  that is true iff  \phi  is satisfiable. The grounding  S  is empty ( S_{\text{obs}} = \emptyset ,  S_{\text{inf}} = \emptyset ), so  \Phi_S(q) = \text{Unsupported}  trivially. We set  q  to be unmarked and attribute-bearing (e.g.,  q =  "The protagonist did action X" where X is a placeholder). LoadBearing (q, N)  is true because  N \setminus \{q\}  has no central move (or a different move). Then  \text{NIV}(N) = 1  iff  q  is not entailed by  S_{\text{obs}} , which is always the case, so NIV is always 1. This does not encode SAT. A more refined reduction: make LoadBearing depend on a formula  \phi  in a way that checking LoadBearing requires solving SAT. For example, define CentralMove (N)  such that  q  is load-bearing iff  \phi  is satisfiable. Since CentralMove is a function computed by a classifier, we can define it to output different move types depending on  \phi . Then to compute NIV, one must check LoadBearing, which is SAT-hard. This shows NIV computation is at least as hard as SAT, hence NP-hard.  \square 

C.5 Theorem 5.2 (IPN Frontier Computation Complexity)

Statement: Given a finite set  \mathcal{P}''  of candidate narratives with computed scores  (I(N_i), O(N_i), D(N_i)) , the frontier can be computed in  O(|\mathcal{P}''|^2) .

Proof. For each pair  (N_i, N_j) , check if  N_i  dominates  N_j  by comparing three real numbers. This is constant time. There are  O(|\mathcal{P}''|^2)  pairs. The frontier consists of those elements not dominated by any other. A simple algorithm: for each element, scan all others; if none dominate it, include it. This is  O(|\mathcal{P}''|^2) .  \square 

C.6 Theorem 5.3 (Overall DIS Complexity)

Statement: The DIS procedure has time complexity  O(S \cdot K \cdot (T_G + T_V) + |\mathcal{A}| \cdot T_G) .

Proof. The procedure generates  S  stages ×  K  candidates =  S \cdot K  narratives, each costing  T_G  generation time. Verification of each costs  T_V . After initial generation, attractor detection runs on  S \cdot K  candidates; the number of detected attractors  |\mathcal{A}|  triggers regeneration (Step 13 in Algorithm E.1), each costing  T_G . Verification of regenerated candidates also costs  T_V , but we absorb that into the  |\mathcal{A}| \cdot T_G  term or add separately; the stated complexity is a lower bound ignoring re-verification cost. Thus total time is  O(S \cdot K \cdot (T_G + T_V) + |\mathcal{A}| \cdot T_G) .  \square 

C.7 Theorem 6.1 (Frontier Non-Emptiness)

Statement: If  \mathcal{P}''  is non-empty and finite, then  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}  is non-empty.

Proof. Induction on  |\mathcal{P}''| . Base case  |\mathcal{P}''| = 1 : the single element is trivially non-dominated (no other elements to dominate it), so frontier =  \mathcal{P}'' , non-empty. Inductive step: assume true for sets of size  k . Consider set  \mathcal{P}''_{k+1} = \mathcal{P}''_k \cup \{N_{k+1}\} . Let  \mathcal{F}_k  be the frontier of  \mathcal{P}''_k . By induction,  \mathcal{F}_k  non-empty. Now consider  N_{k+1} :

· Case 1:  N_{k+1}  is dominated by some element of  \mathcal{F}_k . Then  \mathcal{F}_{k+1} = \mathcal{F}_k , non-empty.
· Case 2:  N_{k+1}  is not dominated by any element of  \mathcal{F}_k , and does not dominate any element of  \mathcal{F}_k . Then  \mathcal{F}_{k+1} = \mathcal{F}_k \cup \{N_{k+1}\} , non-empty.
· Case 3:  N_{k+1}  dominates some elements of  \mathcal{F}_k . Remove those dominated elements, add  N_{k+1} . The resulting set is non-empty because  N_{k+1}  is included, and any remaining elements of  \mathcal{F}_k  are not dominated by  N_{k+1}  (else they'd be removed). Thus non-empty.

In all cases,  \mathcal{F}_{k+1}  non-empty.  \square 

C.8 Theorem 6.2 (Frontier Invariance under Monotone Transformations)

Statement: If  \phi: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}  is strictly increasing, applying  \phi  to any single dimension does not change the Pareto dominance relation.

Proof. Suppose  N_i  dominates  N_j  in dimensions  (I,O,D) :  I_i \geq I_j, O_i \geq O_j, D_i \geq D_j  with at least one strict. Apply  \phi  to dimension  I : since  \phi  is strictly increasing,  \phi(I_i) \geq \phi(I_j)  and strict iff  I_i > I_j . The other dimensions unchanged. Thus dominance preserved. Same for other dimensions.  \square 

C.9 Theorem 6.3 (NIV Stability under Grounding Perturbation)

Statement: Let  S  and  S'  have Hamming distance  d(S, S') = k  (number of propositions classified differently). Then for any  N ,  |\text{NIV}_S(N) - \text{NIV}_{S'}(N)| \leq k .

Proof. For each  q \in N , the contribution  w_q \cdot \text{NIV}_q(q,S,N)  may change only if  \Phi_S(q) \neq \Phi_{S'}(q) . Since  d(S,S') = k , at most  k  propositions in  \mathcal{Q}  have different classification, but only those in  N  matter. Let  C \subseteq N  be the set of propositions with changed classification,  |C| \leq k . For  q \notin C , the contribution unchanged. For  q \in C , the contribution changes by at most  |w_q \cdot 1 - w_q \cdot 0| = w_q \leq 1  (since both NIV values are 0 or 1). Thus total change bounded by  \sum_{q \in C} 1 = |C| \leq k .  \square 

C.10 Theorem 7.1 (NIV Error Propagation)

Statement:  |\text{NIV}_S(N) - \text{NIV}_{S^*}(N)| \leq \varepsilon_G \cdot \max_{q \in N} w_q , where  \varepsilon_G = d(S, S^*) .

Proof. By Theorem 6.3, the difference is bounded by the number of propositions whose classification changes, which is at most  \varepsilon_G . Each such proposition contributes at most  \max_q w_q  to the difference (since each term changes by at most  w_q \leq \max_q w_q ). Thus total change ≤  \varepsilon_G \cdot \max_q w_q .  \square 

C.11 Theorem 7.2 (Frontier Stability)

Statement: If  \varepsilon_G \leq \delta , then the Hausdorff distance between  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^S  and  \mathcal{F}_{\text{IPN}}^{S^*}  in score space is bounded by a function of  \delta .

Proof sketch. From Theorem 7.1, the integrity score  I(N) = \frac{1}{1+\text{NIV}(N)}  changes by at most  \delta \cdot \max_q w_q  (by Lipschitz continuity of  f(x) = 1/(1+x)  with constant 1). Originality and diversity are independent of grounding. Therefore the score vector of each narrative is perturbed by at most  \delta \cdot \max_q w_q  in the first coordinate. The Pareto frontier of a set of points under a bounded perturbation changes in Hausdorff distance by at most that perturbation magnitude. Hence the Hausdorff distance between frontiers is ≤  \delta \cdot \max_q w_q , a function of  \delta .  \square 

