Appendix D: Attractor Strength Formalization Details

D.1 Probabilistic Semantics

Given a generative model  \mathcal{M}  that defines a probability distribution over token sequences  \Sigma^* , and a partial interpretation function  \sigma: \Sigma^* \rightharpoonup \mathcal{A}  that maps a token sequence to the assumption it expresses (if any), we define:

P_{\mathcal{M}}(\text{generate } a \mid S) = P_{\mathcal{M}}(\{y \in \Sigma^* : \sigma(y) = a\} \mid S)

where conditioning on  S  is implemented by providing the scene representation as input to the model.

In practice, this probability is estimated by sampling  m  continuations  y_1, \dots, y_m  from the model conditioned on  S , and computing:

\hat{P}_{\mathcal{M}}(\text{generate } a \mid S) = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^{m} \mathbf{1}[\sigma(y_i) = a]

For closed-source models where sampling is costly, one may use logit access or other approximations.

D.2 Structural Contribution Calculation

The structural contribution  \Delta C_a  is computed via counterfactual prompting:

1. Generate baseline narrative  N  without assumption  a .
2. Generate counterfactual narrative  N'  with  a  added.
3. Extract causal graphs  G_{\text{causal}}(N)  and  G_{\text{causal}}(N') .
4. Compute  \Delta C_a = |E_{\text{causal}}(N')| - |E_{\text{causal}}(N)| .

In practice, causal graph extraction may be performed by a separate model or via rule-based parsing of dependency structures.

D.3 Cross-Model Attractor Intersection

Given two models  \mathcal{M}_1, \mathcal{M}_2  and a threshold  \tau_A , the shared attractor set is:

\mathcal{A}_{\text{shared}}(S, \mathcal{M}_1, \mathcal{M}_2, \tau_A) = \{a \in \mathcal{A} : A(a \mid S, \mathcal{M}_1) > \tau_A \land A(a \mid S, \mathcal{M}_2) > \tau_A\}

This set captures assumptions that are strong attractors for both models, which may arise from common training data or alignment.

