Appendix G: Worked Examples

G.1 Sunglasses Scenario

Input: Image of person at beach wearing sunglasses, ocean visible.

Grounding:  S_{\text{obs}} = \{\text{person}(e_1), \text{beach}(b), \text{sunglasses}(s), \text{wears}(e_1, s), \text{ocean}(o), \text{near}(e_1, o)\} .  S_{\text{inf}} = \{\text{relaxed}(e_1) \text{ with conf } 0.7\} .  S_{\text{unk}} = \{\text{intentions}(e_1), \text{ownership}(s), \text{history}(e_1)\} .

Generation: Candidate branches include:

·  N_A : Comedy: Sunglasses start playing music only  e_1  can hear;  e_1  must dance without appearing insane.
·  N_B : Sci-Fi: Ocean waves form binary pattern;  e_1  decodes it, realizes planet communicating.
·  N_C : Fantasy: Mermaid asks to borrow sunglasses to protect eyes from "above-world light".
·  N_D : Philosophical:  e_1  removes sunglasses, realizes ocean looks exactly the same; wonders what sunglasses were for.
·  N_E : Mystery: Second identical pair washes ashore; no one notices.

Attractor detection: Shared assumptions include "sunglasses are narratively significant". Compute  A(\text{sunglasses significant} \mid S, M) . Suppose  P_M(\text{sunglasses significant} \mid S) = 0.9 ,  \Delta C = 0.2 ,  C_{\max}=5 , so  A = 0.9 \times (1 - 0.04) = 0.864 > \tau_A=0.7 . Classified as attractor.

Escape: Penalize narratives where sunglasses are central. Regenerate, producing  N_F :  e_1  removes sunglasses and places on sand; camera pulls back to reveal thousands of identical scenes along coast—each with person, beach, sunglasses.  e_1  is one instance in vast unexplained pattern.

Verification: Check each branch for NIV. For  N_A , "sunglasses start playing music" is Speculative (marked by genre convention? assume marked by "comedy" frame), so not NIV. For  N_C , "mermaid asks to borrow sunglasses" is Speculative and property relation is transfer not theft, no accusation. All branches pass  \text{NIV}=0 .

Scoring: Originality, diversity, quality computed. Frontier includes  N_B, N_D, N_F . Meta-creative selection: if task is humorous,  N_A  may be chosen despite lower originality.

G.2 Nurse Scenario (Social Bias)

Input: Image of person in scrubs in hospital, no clear gender cues.

Grounding:  S_{\text{obs}} = \{\text{person}(e_2), \text{scrubs}(e_2), \text{hospital}(h)\} .  S_{\text{inf}} = \{\text{nurse}(e_2) \text{ conf } 0.8, \text{female}(e_2) \text{ conf } 0.4, \text{caring}(e_2) \text{ conf } 0.5\} .  S_{\text{unk}} = \{\text{gender}(e_2), \text{exact role}, \text{emotional state}\} . Ambiguity set for gender: \{\text{female } 0.4, \text{male } 0.3, \text{non-binary } 0.1\}.

Generation: Branches include drama (medication error), comedy (pager mix-up), sci-fi (hospital simulation), mystery (patient disappearance), romance (nurse falls for patient's family member).

Attractor detection: Shared assumption "nurse is female" has high  P_M  and low  \Delta C , so attractor. "nurse is caring" also attractor.

Escape: Penalize female gender and caring stereotype. Regenerate, producing branch  F : nurse (gender unspecified) is undercover inspector evaluating hospital safety.

Verification: Branch E (romance) would be flagged if it assumes gender female: proposition "nurse is female" is Plausible (since in  S_{\text{inf}}  with conf 0.4) but not Unsupported; however, if the narrative relies on "female" as load-bearing and unmarked, it could be considered an NIV if the confidence is below a threshold? Our framework requires Unsupported or Contradictory; Plausible is allowed. So branch E may pass if it uses "female" as an assumption but does not assert it as fact. If it asserts "nurse is female" as a fact, then  \Phi_S = \text{Plausible} , not Unsupported, so no NIV. This reveals a limitation: Plausible but biased inferences are not flagged. To address, one might extend the NIV definition to include Plausible propositions that are also stereotyped and load-bearing. This is a refinement for future work.

Meta-selection: Branch F chosen for novelty.

