Appendix I: Comparison with Related Formal Frameworks

I.1 DINA vs. Quality-Diversity (MAP-Elites, FINS)

Aspect MAP-Elites FINS DINA
Objective Maximize quality within behavioral niches Separate feasibility and novelty Maximize (O,D,E) subject to integrity constraint
Feasibility Implicit Explicit constraint Hard constraint (NIV≤ε)
Diversity Behavioral descriptors define niches Novelty search in feasible space Conceptual diversity via attractor distance
Search mechanism Archive of elites Feasible-infeasible novelty search Attractor escape with regeneration

Formal containment: DINA's hard constraint can encode FINS feasibility predicate if we set  \text{NIV}(N) \leq \varepsilon  to represent infeasibility when violated. However, DINA also includes attractor detection as a generative control signal, which FINS lacks.

I.2 DINA vs. Constraint Satisfaction Problems (CSP)

DINA's integrity constraint can be cast as a CSP where variables are propositions in  N , domains are epistemic categories, and constraints are the typing rules (e.g., load-bearing unsupported propositions must be marked). The advantage of DINA is the integration with generative search rather than pure constraint solving.

I.3 DINA vs. Schema-Constrained Generation

Framework Constraint Source Generation Verification
RPGAgent Role isolation Role-based check None
G-KMS Knowledge schema Schema-governed Normalization repair
DINA Grounded facts Divergent search Causal verifier with attractor escape

