Appendix K: Cross-Domain Applications

K.1 Scientific Hypothesis Generation

Domain: Given experimental observation, generate hypotheses.

Application: Grounding extracts observed conditions and results. Divergent generation proposes multiple hypotheses. Attractor detection flags "obvious" hypotheses (e.g., experimental error). Causal verification checks logical consistency with observations. Meta-selection chooses between conservative and bold hypotheses.

Example: Observation "plant grows faster under red light":

· Attractor: "red light = photosynthesis"
· Divergent: "red light affects circadian rhythm gene expression"
· Divergent: "red light changes soil microbiome"

K.2 Autonomous Agent Planning

Domain: Agent plans actions in environment.

Application: Grounding extracts observed state. Divergent generation proposes multiple action sequences. Attractor detection identifies "obvious" plans (direct path to goal). Causal verification checks physical feasibility. Meta-selection chooses between safe and exploratory plans.

K.3 Social Simulation

Domain: Simulate social scenarios.

Application: Grounding extracts social roles and relationships. Divergent generation proposes interaction trajectories. Attractor detection identifies stereotypical interactions. Causal verification checks social plausibility. Meta-selection chooses between realistic and counterfactual scenarios.

